{"name":"Upscale","hex":"#a8adc2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"upscale","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Expensive and designed to appeal to affluent consumers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To increase in size, to scale up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/upscale"}],"entries":[{"title":"Image scaling","blocks":[{"text":"In computer graphics and digital imaging, image scaling is the resizing of a digital image. In video technology, the magnification of digital material is known as upscaling or resolution enhancement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/2xsai_example.svg/330px-2xsai_example.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_scaling"},{"title":"Luxury goods","blocks":[{"text":"In economics, a luxury good is a product or item or service for which demand increases more than what is proportional as income rises, so that expenditures on the good become a more significant proportion of overall spending. Luxury goods are in contrast to necessity goods, where demand increases proportionally less than income. Whereas people consume necessity goods to meet basic survival needs, luxury goods are consumed both for their \"intrinsic quality and to signal their wealth and confirm social status.\" In economics, luxury goods is often used synonymously with superior goods."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Foie_gras_with_sauternes.jpg/330px-Foie_gras_with_sauternes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luxury_goods"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/160_by_160_thumbnail_of_%27Green_Sea_Shell%27.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a6/160_by_160_thumbn